# tinyasr bundles one thing that is not ours:

1. The engine, built from https://github.com/dkaraush/tinyasr
   (GPL-3.0-or-later). scripts/build-engine.sh fetches the pinned revision,
   compiles it for Android arm64 and stages:

     - app/src/main/cpp/prebuilt/arm64-v8a/libtinyasr.a  (the static library)
     - third_party/tinyasr/                              (source + header)
     - app/src/full/assets/models/*                      (only for the "full" flavor)

   The repository also ships the model packs themselves, so the "full" flavor
   can bundle them. If you redistribute the app, the GPL applies to the
   combined work; see third_party/tinyasr/LICENSE.

2. Model packs (en-fa.tzf, en-ru-uk.tzf, silero_vad.onnx) — from the same
   repository. The "core" flavor ships none: import them yourself.

The old approach — loading Telegram's monolithic libtmessages.49.so — was
dropped: its JNI_OnLoad resolves methods on Telegram's own Java classes
(org/telegram/messenger/AnimatedFileDrawableStream and others), which any app
that does not ship them cannot satisfy. The JNI lookup then fails fatally the
moment the library is loaded, which is exactly what "crash while transcribing"
was: the process died in System.loadLibrary, before a single byte of a model
was parsed. See docs/FORENSIC.md.

The app code (dev.tinyasr.app) is provided as-is for personal experimentation.
